This repository contains no code, data or text copied from other projects. All code and all
questions in data/default_suite.json were written for this project.

Ideas it builds on, with thanks:

- livenerf (https://github.com/ninjahawk/livenerf). No code from livenerf is included in this
  repository, because the livenerf repository has no LICENSE file (checked on GitHub on
  2026-10-02; its README states MIT). What was taken are ideas described in its public README:
  compare a model with its own launch-time baseline item by item; grade exactly, never with a
  model; treat output-token count as an early signal; publish the decision rule in advance.
- "Adding Error Bars to Evals" (arXiv:2411.00640, 2024): paired, per-question
  comparison so question difficulty drops out.
- EleutherAI lm-evaluation-harness (MIT) and promptfoo (MIT): deterministic answer extraction and
  assertion-style graders (exact, regex, JSON). No code reused.
- openai/human-eval (MIT): grading code answers by running hidden tests in a separate process
  with a timeout. Our checker is a separate implementation.
- UK AISI Inspect (MIT): logging every sample with usage, as the unit of later analysis.
- NerfWatch (MIT, https://github.com/tendaigomo/NerfWatch): tracking categories separately so a
  collapse in one area cannot hide behind the average.
- is-gpt-nerfed (MIT, https://github.com/kiyoakii/is-gpt-nerfed): checking which model actually
  answered, and treating "no usable answer" as invalid rather than as a verdict.
- llm-health-monitor (MIT, https://github.com/hajunho/llm-health-monitor): separating provider
  outages from quality changes.

Statistical methods (hypergeometric conditional test, Holm-Bonferroni, exact sign test,
percentile bootstrap) are textbook methods implemented from scratch with the Python standard
library.
